1
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:14,100
Hello and welcome to the last lecture in our introduction to photonics video series.

2
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:22,100
This is going to be our last lecture, and in a sense is a segue to other courses in this online curriculum.

3
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:29,100
We started playing with lasers, and as you acquired certain skills in photonics,

4
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:33,100
you will be exposed to lasers that have a higher power,

5
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:37,100
so the whole issue of laser safety starts playing an important role.

6
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:41,100
In the last lecture we talked about anatomy of a human eye,

7
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:49,100
and we've seen that laser power, power from the lasers, poses a hazard to the safety of the human eye.

8
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:54,100
So this lecture, lecture number eight, or the last lecture in this video series,

9
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:58,100
is going to be dedicated to the lasers and the laser safety.

10
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:06,100
So in this lecture we are going to describe the laser classifications based on certain standards established by authorities.

11
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:10,100
We are going to define a concept called maximum permissible exposure,

12
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:14,100
a concept that plays an important role in laser safety.

13
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:18,100
We are going to talk about three general types of laser hazard controls.

14
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:30,100
We'll mention and show a few warning signs that are used to indicate the laser safety hazards.

15
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:35,100
We are also going to address the most frequent causes of laser accidents

16
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:41,100
and finally we are going to talk about laser safety precautions that you as photonics technicians

17
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:45,100
should take into account when you're working with lasers.

18
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:51,100
So the need to address this issue has been established already back in the 70s,

19
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:57,100
so the last century when lasers had been invented and started being used.

20
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:03,100
And the first standard that has been established is back in 1969.

21
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:11,100
It's been established at the request of the U.S. Department of Labor and addressed the issue of the laser safety.

22
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:19,100
This original laser safety standard has been updated a few times since present time.

23
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:24,100
For example, in 1973 another standard has been established and directed primarily to users

24
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:29,100
such as operators, technicians, laser safety officers, safety engineers, and research personnel.

25
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:35,100
And then there has been a few more updates in 1980, 1986, 1993.

26
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:38,100
There was a revision back in 2000.

27
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:42,100
So as lasers are evolving, as lasers are being developed,

28
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:48,100
so do the laser safety standards that are being evolved to address specific issues

29
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:55,100
that are related to the new developments in the whole area of laser safety.

30
00:02:55,100 --> 00:03:01,100
So the primary laser safety standard for the industry is so-called NCZ136.1.

31
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:06,100
And then there's also a specific type of laser safety standard for educational institutions,

32
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:11,100
so-called NCA136.5.

33
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:16,100
These standards can be obtained by different institutions,

34
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:21,100
specifically the Laser Institute of America that's been stationed in Orlando, Florida,

35
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:26,100
and can give you access to this type of documents.

36
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:29,100
And also they may be available also online.

37
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:37,100
You can purchase these documents that are having a pretty large number of pages

38
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:41,100
that you would have to read, especially if you are to be a certified

39
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:44,100
as a laser safety officer or a person who's going to be responsible

40
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:48,100
for the safety in a photonics or laser labs.

41
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:51,100
You will have to read it through these documents and you will have to study

42
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:54,100
in detail all the aspects of laser safety.

43
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:59,100
So according to laser safety standards, lasers are classified into a few groups

44
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:04,100
based on the potential hazards that they pose.

45
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:08,100
So we are going to start with the class I, so-called exempt lasers.

46
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:13,100
This type of lasers, this class of lasers cannot, under normal operating conditions,

47
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:16,100
emit a hazardous level of optical radiation.

48
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:20,100
And the lasers that are included in this category is certain lasers

49
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:24,100
that are part of the laboratory equipment with all beam paths

50
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:26,100
and reflections that are enclosed.

51
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:32,100
So you may have other lasers of a higher class, like class IV or class III,

52
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:36,100
that can be classified as a class I

53
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:41,100
if there's a proper enclosure that has been put around that type of a laser.

54
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:48,100
The next in the list of classifications of lasers is so-called class II.

55
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:53,100
Here we are talking about low power visible laser devices of about 1 milliwatt.

56
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:58,100
So these types of lasers would not have enough output power to injure a person

57
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:04,100
accidentally, but may injure the eye if it stayed for a long period of time.

58
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:07,100
So that's another important aspect that has to be mentioned.

59
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:10,100
So it's not just about the power or irradiance of the laser.

60
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:14,100
It's also about exposure time.

61
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:18,100
So you will see that you have to take into account

62
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:23,100
both the power of the laser and exposure time in order to properly quantify

63
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:29,100
whether a laser is posing any kind of threat or risk for laser safety.

64
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:32,100
So back to class number II.

65
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:38,100
Class II usually is going to be labeled with a caution sign

66
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:41,100
that has to be put on a device.

67
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:46,100
Types of lasers that would go under the class II would be many helium neon lasers,

68
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:48,100
not all of them.

69
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:53,100
And these types of lasers are usually used for alignment procedures and optical experiments.

70
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:56,100
So if you have a higher power type of a laser,

71
00:05:56,100 --> 00:06:00,100
you don't want to fool around with that type of laser during the alignment procedure.

72
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:04,100
So the way how you're aligning a higher class laser

73
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:08,100
is basically having another laser that's part of the same laser enclosure

74
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:14,100
that's visible and that's classified as class II, which doesn't pose the risk.

75
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:16,100
So you're going to do the whole alignment procedure.

76
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:19,100
You're going to align the beam of the class II laser,

77
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:21,100
and then you're going to turn off that laser

78
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:26,100
and then turn on the high power laser of class III or class IV.

79
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:29,100
That's going to be the main laser for your experiment.

80
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:32,100
Next class is class IIIa.

81
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:37,100
Here we are talking about power levels of 1 milliwatt up to 5 milliwatts.

82
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:41,100
These types of lasers cannot injure a normal person

83
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:43,100
when viewed with an unaided eye,

84
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:45,100
but may cause injury when energy is collected

85
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:49,100
and put into the eye using certain focusing optics,

86
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:52,100
such as binoculars, etc.

87
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:57,100
So in this class, most laser pointers will be classified,

88
00:06:57,100 --> 00:07:01,100
and in this case, you would want to put a danger or caution sign

89
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:04,100
on the device depending on the irradiance,

90
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:09,100
depending on the amount of power that that laser is going to radiate.

91
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:12,100
Next class is class IIIb.

92
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:16,100
Here we start getting serious in terms of the laser safety.

93
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:21,100
We're talking about power levels from 5 milliwatts to 500 milliwatt.

94
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:25,100
These lasers can produce eye injury when viewed without eye protection.

95
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:31,100
So if you are experimenting, if you're working with class IIIb and class IV lasers,

96
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:35,100
you have to wear laser eye protection at all times.

97
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:38,100
So this class of laser would require a danger label

98
00:07:38,100 --> 00:07:41,100
and could have a dangerous specular reflection,

99
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:46,100
meaning not only that this laser poses the safety risk

100
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:50,100
if your eyes are exposed directly to the laser beam,

101
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:55,100
so if laser beam is directed towards the certain reflecting surface

102
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:59,100
that can reflect the laser beam,

103
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:01,100
especially specular reflections or reflections

104
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:06,100
that are not being scattered or diffused in many directions.

105
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:11,100
So as already mentioned, if you are working with class IIIb laser,

106
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:14,100
you have to wear the eye protection at all times.

107
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:18,100
And finally, the highest class is class IV.

108
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:22,100
We are talking about lasers that have powers above half a watt

109
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:24,100
or above 500 milliwatts.

110
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:27,100
These types of lasers can injure you if viewed directly

111
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:30,100
or by viewing both the specular or diffused reflections.

112
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:34,100
So here it's not just the specular but also diffused reflections.

113
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:37,100
The engine side has to label this type of a laser

114
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:41,100
and some of these lasers can also present a tire hazard.

115
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:44,100
And in this case, both eye and skin protection

116
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:47,100
would be required in operating this type of a laser.

117
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:51,100
So as this slide shows, lasers, according to laser safety standards,

118
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:55,100
are going to be classified in different classes starting with class I

119
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:57,100
and going all the way to class IV

120
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:59,100
from the lowest possible power levels

121
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:02,100
all the way to the highest possible power levels.

122
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:08,100
You also may come across a few other classification updates recently

123
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:12,100
that we are going to talk in more in detail

124
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:14,100
in a high power laser class

125
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:17,100
that's going to be part of this online curriculum.

126
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:24,100
Now that we familiar ourselves with different safety classes,

127
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:27,100
classifications of lasers, we also want to talk about

128
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:30,100
what type of safety controls would have to be established

129
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:35,100
in a lab or in a space in which you're going to be operating

130
00:09:35,100 --> 00:09:38,100
class III and class IV type of lasers.

131
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:44,100
So the main focus here is on the class IIIb and class IV lasers

132
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:48,100
that operate at the power levels above half a watt.

133
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:51,100
And if you're operating these types of lasers,

134
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:56,100
you definitely have to have a very explicitly defined

135
00:09:56,100 --> 00:09:59,100
type of laser safety controls in the lab

136
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:03,100
that would prevent any kind of injuries to your eye and to your skin.

137
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:06,100
There's a few different types of control measures

138
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:08,100
according to the laser safety standards.

139
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:12,100
Control measures can be broken down into three main types.

140
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:16,100
You may establish engineering controls.

141
00:10:16,100 --> 00:10:18,100
You may have administrative controls.

142
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:22,100
And finally, you may wear personal protected equipment

143
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:25,100
or popularly known as PPE.

144
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:28,100
So when we are talking about engineering controls,

145
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:31,100
we are talking about certain design features or devices.

146
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:33,100
So we are talking about hardware in this case.

147
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:37,100
So certain design features or devices that are applied

148
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:40,100
to the laser, laser beam or laser environment,

149
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:43,100
that would restrict exposure or reduce irradiance.

150
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:46,100
So what kind of controls are we talking about here?

151
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:47,100
We're talking about beam shutters.

152
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:49,100
We're talking about beam attenuators,

153
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:52,100
remote firing and monitoring systems,

154
00:10:52,100 --> 00:10:56,100
and also protective housing placed entirely around some laser systems.

155
00:10:56,100 --> 00:11:00,100
So very often, laser labs would have interlocks.

156
00:11:00,100 --> 00:11:06,100
The doors would be carefully controlled.

157
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:09,100
There would have to be some sort of laser sign,

158
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:13,100
laser safety light outside that would be turned on

159
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:15,100
during the operation of the laser.

160
00:11:15,100 --> 00:11:18,100
And if someone opens the door during the laser operation,

161
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:22,100
there would have to be some sort of automatic shutdown of the laser.

162
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:26,100
So we are talking about a lot of hardware

163
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:33,100
that would be used to reduce any kind of risk of laser safety,

164
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:38,100
of laser injury during the operation of the laser.

165
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:41,100
Not only are we talking about monitoring systems,

166
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:44,100
we're also talking about certain features,

167
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:46,100
such as certain walls, beam shutters.

168
00:11:46,100 --> 00:11:48,100
You know, you may have a beam dumps

169
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:51,100
that would be collecting the laser radiation.

170
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:52,100
You may have a beam attenuators

171
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:54,100
that would be reducing the amount of power

172
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:57,100
that's coming out of the laser, et cetera.

173
00:11:57,100 --> 00:12:02,100
So a lot of different types of engineering controls

174
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:04,100
that can be established in the laser lab.

175
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:08,100
Next, a group is so-called administrative control measures.

176
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:10,100
Here we are talking about certain procedures.

177
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:12,100
We are talking about the information,

178
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:15,100
rather than devices and mechanical systems.

179
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:18,100
So important administrative controls

180
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:21,100
would be posting warning signs and labels,

181
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:25,100
establishment of SOPs or standard operating procedures,

182
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:27,100
safety training.

183
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:29,100
Very often people who are entering the lab

184
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:33,100
would have to sign in with a specific date and time

185
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:34,100
when they entered the lab,

186
00:12:34,100 --> 00:12:38,100
and they also have to sign out when they are leaving the lab.

187
00:12:38,100 --> 00:12:42,100
Very often those types of laser safety books

188
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:46,100
or operating books in the labs

189
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:50,100
would also include different measurements

190
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:56,100
or experiments that have been conducted in the laser lab.

191
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:59,100
So a lot of paperwork that we're talking about here,

192
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:02,100
so we're talking about administrative control measures

193
00:13:02,100 --> 00:13:05,100
that can be established in a laser lab.

194
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:09,100
And finally, the third group of safety controls

195
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:12,100
is so-called PPE, or personal protective equipment.

196
00:13:12,100 --> 00:13:14,100
So here we are talking about equipment

197
00:13:14,100 --> 00:13:17,100
that is worn by the personnel,

198
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:20,100
such as laser technicians, laser safety officers,

199
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:25,100
and other people who will be present in a laser lab.

200
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:27,100
So we're talking about protective eyewear,

201
00:13:27,100 --> 00:13:29,100
we're talking about gloves,

202
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:31,100
we're talking about special clothing,

203
00:13:31,100 --> 00:13:34,100
so anything that can go on an operator

204
00:13:34,100 --> 00:13:38,100
or on a human being that is present in a laser lab

205
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:40,100
is what's going to go

206
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:42,100
under the personal protective equipment.

207
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:47,100
This slide shows in a form of a table

208
00:13:47,100 --> 00:13:51,100
the classifications of different types of safety controls

209
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:53,100
listed in three columns.

210
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:55,100
So starting with the column on the left-hand side,

211
00:13:55,100 --> 00:13:57,100
we have engineering controls.

212
00:13:57,100 --> 00:13:59,100
So under engineering controls,

213
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:03,100
we would list protective housing and service panels,

214
00:14:03,100 --> 00:14:05,100
interlocks on the protective housing,

215
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:08,100
door interlocks, remote control connectors,

216
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:10,100
beam attenuators, beam shutters,

217
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:12,100
key switch and padlocks,

218
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:14,100
filtered viewing optics and windows,

219
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:16,100
emission delay, warning lights,

220
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:18,100
emission indicators, beam enclosures,

221
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:21,100
control beam pads, laser-controlled area,

222
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:24,100
beam stops, remote firing and monitoring.

223
00:14:24,100 --> 00:14:26,100
So a whole bunch of different engineering controls.

224
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:29,100
We are going to study these engineering controls

225
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:32,100
more in detail in the class

226
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:35,100
that is going to be dedicated to the high-power lasers

227
00:14:35,100 --> 00:14:37,100
and laser systems.

228
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:40,100
In the column in the middle,

229
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:43,100
you have administrative and procedural controls,

230
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:46,100
establishment of a laser safety officer,

231
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:49,100
standard operating procedures,

232
00:14:49,100 --> 00:14:51,100
limitations on use by class,

233
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:53,100
entry limitations for visitors,

234
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:55,100
different types of trainings

235
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:58,100
that the operators would have to undergo,

236
00:14:58,100 --> 00:15:00,100
maintenance and service manuals,

237
00:15:00,100 --> 00:15:02,100
marking and protective devices,

238
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:03,100
warning signs and labels.

239
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:06,100
So a lot of administrative stuff.

240
00:15:06,100 --> 00:15:10,100
And finally, PPE or personal protective equipment.

241
00:15:10,100 --> 00:15:13,100
There we would have eyewear,

242
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:15,100
we would have clothing and gloves

243
00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:17,100
in the case of a class for lasers.

244
00:15:17,100 --> 00:15:20,100
There is also a risk to the skin injury.

245
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:23,100
An important concept that has to be introduced

246
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:25,100
when it is about laser safety

247
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:28,100
is a concept of so-called MPE

248
00:15:28,100 --> 00:15:31,100
or maximum permissible exposure.

249
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:36,100
So maximum permissible exposure

250
00:15:36,100 --> 00:15:41,100
represents a threshold that you are going to establish

251
00:15:41,100 --> 00:15:44,100
for every type and class of a laser

252
00:15:44,100 --> 00:15:49,100
that would enable you to quantify

253
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:52,100
whether you are operating in a safe environment

254
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:55,100
or you don't operate in a safe environment.

255
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:58,100
So every laser has a certain output characteristics

256
00:15:58,100 --> 00:16:00,100
such as wavelength, output, energy and power.

257
00:16:00,100 --> 00:16:02,100
The size of the irradiated area

258
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:04,100
and also the duration of the exposure.

259
00:16:04,100 --> 00:16:06,100
And all these different output characteristics

260
00:16:06,100 --> 00:16:09,100
are going to define how much exposure

261
00:16:09,100 --> 00:16:11,100
to laser is hazardous

262
00:16:11,100 --> 00:16:14,100
and that exposure is going to be quantified

263
00:16:14,100 --> 00:16:17,100
to the so-called maximum permissible exposure.

264
00:16:17,100 --> 00:16:20,100
So the maximum permissible exposure

265
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:25,100
is a quantity that gives you the information

266
00:16:25,100 --> 00:16:27,100
about the greatest exposure

267
00:16:27,100 --> 00:16:30,100
that most individuals can tolerate

268
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:32,100
without sustaining injury.

269
00:16:32,100 --> 00:16:35,100
So if you are dealing with a specific type of a laser

270
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:37,100
you would have to analyze

271
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:39,100
and figure out based on the wavelength

272
00:16:39,100 --> 00:16:40,100
and the power levels

273
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:43,100
and also exposure time

274
00:16:43,100 --> 00:16:46,100
would have to determine from certain specific tables

275
00:16:46,100 --> 00:16:48,100
you have to determine the MPE

276
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:50,100
for that specific laser.

277
00:16:50,100 --> 00:16:53,100
And if you are operating that laser

278
00:16:53,100 --> 00:16:55,100
you want to make sure that

279
00:16:55,100 --> 00:16:58,100
the amount of irradiance that goes into your eye

280
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:00,100
is below this threshold

281
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:04,100
below this so-called maximum permissible exposure.

282
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:07,100
If the amount of power that's coming out of the laser

283
00:17:07,100 --> 00:17:10,100
is higher, is above the MPE

284
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:13,100
then you would have to introduce

285
00:17:13,100 --> 00:17:15,100
certain laser safety measures

286
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:19,100
to push the MPE, to push the power of the laser

287
00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:21,100
or the power that goes into your eye

288
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:24,100
below this threshold.

289
00:17:24,100 --> 00:17:26,100
It turns out that MPE

290
00:17:26,100 --> 00:17:28,100
or maximum permissible exposure

291
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:30,100
is going to depend on both

292
00:17:30,100 --> 00:17:32,100
both on incident irradiance level

293
00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:34,100
coming out of the laser

294
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:36,100
and also exposure time.

295
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:39,100
In other words you may have

296
00:17:39,100 --> 00:17:41,100
the same level of risk

297
00:17:41,100 --> 00:17:44,100
if you are handling low power laser

298
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:46,100
over a longer period of time

299
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:49,100
as you would with a high power laser

300
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:51,100
over a shorter period of time.

301
00:17:51,100 --> 00:17:52,100
So you have to take into account

302
00:17:52,100 --> 00:17:57,100
both the incident irradiance level of a laser

303
00:17:57,100 --> 00:17:59,100
and then also exposure time.

304
00:17:59,100 --> 00:18:01,100
The MPE is usually expressed

305
00:18:01,100 --> 00:18:03,100
in terms of allowable exposure time in seconds

306
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:05,100
for a given irradiance.

307
00:18:05,100 --> 00:18:07,100
So the unit that we are going to use

308
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:09,100
is usually watts per centimeter squared

309
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:10,100
at a particular wavelength.

310
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:12,100
So when you are calculating the irradiance

311
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:17,100
of a laser that you are going to operate

312
00:18:17,100 --> 00:18:19,100
you would want to figure out the irradiance

313
00:18:19,100 --> 00:18:20,100
in terms of this unit,

314
00:18:20,100 --> 00:18:22,100
watt per centimeter squared

315
00:18:22,100 --> 00:18:24,100
because that is what you are going to be comparing to.

316
00:18:24,100 --> 00:18:25,100
You want to make sure that you are comparing

317
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:26,100
apples to apples.

318
00:18:26,100 --> 00:18:29,100
In other words you have to compare two numbers

319
00:18:29,100 --> 00:18:32,100
that are represented by the same unit.

320
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:36,100
So let's take a first look at the table

321
00:18:36,100 --> 00:18:39,100
that can be used to determine

322
00:18:39,100 --> 00:18:41,100
the maximum permissible exposure

323
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:43,100
for a specific type of a laser.

324
00:18:43,100 --> 00:18:45,100
So the table is shown here on this slide.

325
00:18:45,100 --> 00:18:47,100
You can see in the left hand column

326
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:48,100
a laser type.

327
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:51,100
So for a specific type of a laser

328
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:53,100
let's say helium neon laser

329
00:18:53,100 --> 00:18:56,100
you can see a specific wavelength

330
00:18:56,100 --> 00:18:58,100
that laser is going to operate.

331
00:18:58,100 --> 00:19:03,100
And then in four columns on the right hand side

332
00:19:03,100 --> 00:19:06,100
you will have the maximum permissible exposure

333
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:08,100
for different exposure time in seconds.

334
00:19:08,100 --> 00:19:10,100
So you can see that for example

335
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:12,100
for helium neon laser

336
00:19:12,100 --> 00:19:15,100
you basically have three numbers.

337
00:19:15,100 --> 00:19:18,100
You have a number for a quarter second

338
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:21,100
and then you have a number for 600 seconds

339
00:19:21,100 --> 00:19:23,100
or about 10 minutes

340
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:26,100
and then you also have a number

341
00:19:26,100 --> 00:19:29,100
for a much larger exposure time

342
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,100
of three times 10 to the four seconds

343
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:34,100
or 30,000 seconds.

344
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:37,100
So these three numbers that you are seeing here

345
00:19:37,100 --> 00:19:38,100
for the helium neon laser

346
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:42,100
represent the maximum permissible exposure

347
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:44,100
for a short period of time

348
00:19:44,100 --> 00:19:45,100
for a medium period of time

349
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:47,100
and also for a long period of time

350
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:49,100
that are going to be used

351
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:52,100
as the threshold or as the MPE

352
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,100
for this specific type of a laser.

353
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:56,100
Let's now go back to the foundation.

354
00:19:56,100 --> 00:19:57,100
So let's talk about the absorption

355
00:19:57,100 --> 00:20:00,100
and the so-called Lambert's law.

356
00:20:00,100 --> 00:20:02,100
So what we are trying to do here

357
00:20:02,100 --> 00:20:06,100
is we are trying to analyze the way

358
00:20:06,100 --> 00:20:10,100
how a laser safety eyewear operates.

359
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:13,100
So this specific optical window

360
00:20:13,100 --> 00:20:16,100
that you are seeing here, a specific thickness

361
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:19,100
is a representation of a laser safety eyewear.

362
00:20:19,100 --> 00:20:22,100
So you can see that on one side

363
00:20:22,100 --> 00:20:23,100
we have a laser beam

364
00:20:23,100 --> 00:20:26,100
that has an incident irradiance level

365
00:20:26,100 --> 00:20:28,100
of E sub zero

366
00:20:28,100 --> 00:20:30,100
and as that laser beam is moving

367
00:20:30,100 --> 00:20:32,100
through the thickness of the optical window

368
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:34,100
which is going to be our laser safety eyewear

369
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:36,100
as already mentioned,

370
00:20:36,100 --> 00:20:39,100
there is going to be absorption taking place

371
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:42,100
so the incident irradiance level

372
00:20:42,100 --> 00:20:44,100
is going to be dropping down

373
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:46,100
as the light in the laser beam

374
00:20:46,100 --> 00:20:49,100
is going to be absorbed by the optical window.

375
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:51,100
So that's what comes over on the other side

376
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:57,100
has significantly lower irradiance level.

377
00:20:57,100 --> 00:21:00,100
So our goal is to reduce

378
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:02,100
the amount of incident irradiance level

379
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:04,100
on the left-hand side

380
00:21:04,100 --> 00:21:05,100
to something that's acceptable

381
00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:07,100
or something that's below

382
00:21:07,100 --> 00:21:09,100
maximum permissible exposure.

383
00:21:09,100 --> 00:21:13,100
So in order to be able to conduct

384
00:21:13,100 --> 00:21:15,100
these types of calculations

385
00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:18,100
to figure out what type of laser safety eyewear

386
00:21:18,100 --> 00:21:21,100
you would want to use in your laser lab

387
00:21:21,100 --> 00:21:23,100
for a specific type of a laser,

388
00:21:23,100 --> 00:21:25,100
we need to go back to the foundations.

389
00:21:25,100 --> 00:21:28,100
We have to analyze so-called Lambert's Law

390
00:21:28,100 --> 00:21:31,100
that basically quantifies

391
00:21:31,100 --> 00:21:37,100
the amount of transmitted irradiance level

392
00:21:37,100 --> 00:21:39,100
at the exit of the optical window

393
00:21:39,100 --> 00:21:43,100
compared to the input in the irradiance level.

394
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:45,100
So what you're seeing on the right-hand side

395
00:21:45,100 --> 00:21:48,100
in the box is a formula or equation

396
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:51,100
that can be used to do those types of calculations.

397
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:53,100
So let's quickly explain this formula.

398
00:21:53,100 --> 00:21:58,100
What you'll see is the factor

399
00:21:58,100 --> 00:22:00,100
on the right-hand side

400
00:22:00,100 --> 00:22:07,100
that has two quantities in an exponent.

401
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:09,100
One quantity is a quantity alpha.

402
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:11,100
That's so-called absorption coefficient.

403
00:22:11,100 --> 00:22:15,100
Absorption coefficient is basically coefficient

404
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:18,100
that determines or quantifies

405
00:22:18,100 --> 00:22:21,100
the absorptive properties of a material.

406
00:22:21,100 --> 00:22:24,100
So for different types of materials

407
00:22:24,100 --> 00:22:26,100
that this optical window is made out of,

408
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:28,100
you're going to have a different absorption coefficient.

409
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:31,100
And then another factor in an exponent

410
00:22:31,100 --> 00:22:33,100
is a factor x, quantity x,

411
00:22:33,100 --> 00:22:37,100
that represents the thickness of the absorbing material

412
00:22:37,100 --> 00:22:39,100
or thickness of this optical window.

413
00:22:39,100 --> 00:22:43,100
So we can see that the output irradiance,

414
00:22:43,100 --> 00:22:46,100
E sub x, the irradiance that's coming at the exit,

415
00:22:46,100 --> 00:22:49,100
that's exiting the optical window,

416
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:52,100
is going to be the function,

417
00:22:52,100 --> 00:22:55,100
is going to be determined by the type of a material

418
00:22:55,100 --> 00:22:57,100
that the optical window is made out of,

419
00:22:57,100 --> 00:22:59,100
thickness of the material,

420
00:22:59,100 --> 00:23:02,100
and finally the input power levels

421
00:23:02,100 --> 00:23:05,100
or input irradiance in a laser beam

422
00:23:05,100 --> 00:23:08,100
that's entering the optical window.

423
00:23:08,100 --> 00:23:11,100
All this kind of makes a common sense, right,

424
00:23:11,100 --> 00:23:14,100
is a common sense because we would expect

425
00:23:14,100 --> 00:23:17,100
that the amount of light that's going to be absorbed

426
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:19,100
by this optical window is going to depend

427
00:23:19,100 --> 00:23:22,100
on the material properties of the optical window

428
00:23:22,100 --> 00:23:24,100
and then also thickness,

429
00:23:24,100 --> 00:23:28,100
because thicker the optical window is,

430
00:23:28,100 --> 00:23:31,100
the light has to go through more of a material,

431
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:33,100
which means that it's going to be more absorption

432
00:23:33,100 --> 00:23:35,100
or the output power levels

433
00:23:35,100 --> 00:23:39,100
or output irradiance levels will be lower.

434
00:23:39,100 --> 00:23:42,100
So once more, the absorption coefficient, alpha,

435
00:23:42,100 --> 00:23:44,100
is a property of the absorbing material

436
00:23:44,100 --> 00:23:46,100
and then also is a function of the wavelength of the light.

437
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:48,100
That's why we have this small lambda

438
00:23:48,100 --> 00:23:51,100
in the subscript of this coefficient.

439
00:23:51,100 --> 00:23:54,100
In other words, the absorption coefficient is going to change

440
00:23:54,100 --> 00:23:56,100
for a specific material

441
00:23:56,100 --> 00:23:58,100
based on the wavelength of the light

442
00:23:58,100 --> 00:24:01,100
that's passing through that type of a material.

443
00:24:01,100 --> 00:24:03,100
So if you're talking about eye filters

444
00:24:03,100 --> 00:24:06,100
or that are used in the laser safety goggles,

445
00:24:06,100 --> 00:24:10,100
the absorption coefficient of the filter is known

446
00:24:10,100 --> 00:24:12,100
and the filter thickness is also predetermined.

447
00:24:12,100 --> 00:24:14,100
So very often we don't have to look at

448
00:24:14,100 --> 00:24:17,100
both of these quantities separately.

449
00:24:17,100 --> 00:24:20,100
In other words, the coefficient alpha

450
00:24:20,100 --> 00:24:23,100
thickness X would not have to be looked separately

451
00:24:23,100 --> 00:24:26,100
but can be replaced by a single quantity,

452
00:24:26,100 --> 00:24:28,100
that so-called optical density,

453
00:24:28,100 --> 00:24:31,100
also very popularly known as OD.

454
00:24:31,100 --> 00:24:34,100
So every type of laser safety goggles

455
00:24:34,100 --> 00:24:40,100
is going to be defined by a specific OD number

456
00:24:40,100 --> 00:24:43,100
or so-called optical density.

457
00:24:43,100 --> 00:24:48,100
So if we are using that single quantity,

458
00:24:48,100 --> 00:24:53,100
our formula is going to be slightly updated,

459
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:56,100
as you can see on the right-hand side.

460
00:24:56,100 --> 00:24:59,100
We are not going to be using the natural base E,

461
00:24:59,100 --> 00:25:02,100
but we're going to be using the base 10

462
00:25:02,100 --> 00:25:05,100
in our factor on the right-hand side.

463
00:25:05,100 --> 00:25:08,100
And then also instead of using two quantities,

464
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:10,100
the coefficient alpha and the thickness X,

465
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:12,100
we're going to be using one single quantity OD

466
00:25:12,100 --> 00:25:16,100
that is going to determine the optical properties

467
00:25:16,100 --> 00:25:20,100
of the eye filter or laser safety goggles

468
00:25:20,100 --> 00:25:23,100
that are going to be used.

469
00:25:23,100 --> 00:25:25,100
So the optical density by itself

470
00:25:25,100 --> 00:25:27,100
is also a wave-independent quantity

471
00:25:27,100 --> 00:25:31,100
for a simple reason of it being a function

472
00:25:31,100 --> 00:25:34,100
of the coefficient alpha.

473
00:25:34,100 --> 00:25:37,100
And usually optical density is an important quantity

474
00:25:37,100 --> 00:25:39,100
that's going to be labeled on the temple of the goggles

475
00:25:39,100 --> 00:25:41,100
or on the filter itself.

476
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:43,100
So any type of laser safety goggles

477
00:25:43,100 --> 00:25:45,100
that you're going to be using in Laser Lab,

478
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:50,100
you would want to look at the top corner

479
00:25:50,100 --> 00:25:53,100
of most of time of those laser safety goggles

480
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:55,100
and identify what would be the optical density

481
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:57,100
for a specific wavelength range.

482
00:25:57,100 --> 00:26:00,100
Very often there's going to be multiple wavelength ranges

483
00:26:00,100 --> 00:26:03,100
that are going to be covered by those laser safety goggles

484
00:26:03,100 --> 00:26:07,100
with different OD numbers.

485
00:26:07,100 --> 00:26:09,100
So that's an important property

486
00:26:09,100 --> 00:26:12,100
of any pair of laser safety goggles

487
00:26:12,100 --> 00:26:17,100
that you would want to look at during your determination

488
00:26:17,100 --> 00:26:20,100
of whether those laser safety goggles are proper to be used

489
00:26:20,100 --> 00:26:24,100
for a specific type of laser that you're operating.

490
00:26:24,100 --> 00:26:26,100
So here we want to put into perspective

491
00:26:26,100 --> 00:26:30,100
what this number OD really means.

492
00:26:30,100 --> 00:26:32,100
So usually it's going to be some sort of integer.

493
00:26:32,100 --> 00:26:36,100
So we want to kind of really understand physically

494
00:26:36,100 --> 00:26:39,100
what a specific OD number means.

495
00:26:39,100 --> 00:26:43,100
So on the right-hand side you have a table.

496
00:26:43,100 --> 00:26:45,100
On the left-hand column of this table,

497
00:26:45,100 --> 00:26:47,100
you can see different OD numbers

498
00:26:47,100 --> 00:26:49,100
starting with zero on the top

499
00:26:49,100 --> 00:26:51,100
and going all the way down to number seven,

500
00:26:51,100 --> 00:26:53,100
OD number seven.

501
00:26:53,100 --> 00:26:55,100
Now what, for example,

502
00:26:55,100 --> 00:26:58,100
OD of three really means is

503
00:26:58,100 --> 00:27:02,100
if you go to the formula on the right-hand side,

504
00:27:02,100 --> 00:27:07,100
you can substitute three for the OD for three.

505
00:27:07,100 --> 00:27:10,100
So you're going to have ten to the negative three,

506
00:27:10,100 --> 00:27:14,100
which is nothing else than 0.001 or 0.1%,

507
00:27:14,100 --> 00:27:19,100
which means that the laser safety eyewear

508
00:27:19,100 --> 00:27:22,100
that has OD number equal to three

509
00:27:22,100 --> 00:27:25,100
is going to be passing only 0.1%

510
00:27:25,100 --> 00:27:28,100
of the original input irradiance level

511
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:33,100
that's entering the type of laser safety eyewear.

512
00:27:33,100 --> 00:27:35,100
So you can kind of put physically into perspective

513
00:27:35,100 --> 00:27:37,100
what these different OD numbers mean.

514
00:27:37,100 --> 00:27:40,100
So obviously higher the OD is,

515
00:27:40,100 --> 00:27:43,100
less power or less irradiance is going to be pushed

516
00:27:43,100 --> 00:27:45,100
through the laser safety eyewear.

517
00:27:45,100 --> 00:27:50,100
So also if you're using a laser with higher power levels,

518
00:27:50,100 --> 00:27:52,100
you would have to reduce the amount of power

519
00:27:52,100 --> 00:27:55,100
that's getting out of the laser safety goggles

520
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:57,100
by using the laser safety goggles

521
00:27:57,100 --> 00:27:59,100
that have a higher OD number.

522
00:27:59,100 --> 00:28:03,100
So very simple equation that's shown in the box

523
00:28:03,100 --> 00:28:06,100
on the right-hand side is where you can convert

524
00:28:06,100 --> 00:28:09,100
the specific OD number into the percentage

525
00:28:09,100 --> 00:28:14,100
of the transmission through that type of laser safety eyewear.

526
00:28:14,100 --> 00:28:19,100
And then finally, this slide here is a graphic representation

527
00:28:19,100 --> 00:28:25,100
of how laser safety goggles are operating.

528
00:28:25,100 --> 00:28:27,100
So on the left-hand side, you have a laser

529
00:28:27,100 --> 00:28:30,100
that's emitting certain laser radiation

530
00:28:30,100 --> 00:28:33,100
that's here represented by the incident irradiance level

531
00:28:33,100 --> 00:28:35,100
of E sub zero.

532
00:28:35,100 --> 00:28:39,100
It turns out that E sub zero for this specific type of laser

533
00:28:39,100 --> 00:28:43,100
is above the NPE, acceptable NPE level.

534
00:28:43,100 --> 00:28:45,100
So you would have to use some sort

535
00:28:45,100 --> 00:28:47,100
of laser safety protection.

536
00:28:47,100 --> 00:28:49,100
So here we are going to use laser safety protection

537
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:51,100
that's shown here as this filter

538
00:28:51,100 --> 00:28:53,100
that's going to have a certain absorptive properties.

539
00:28:53,100 --> 00:28:55,100
So this incident irradiance level that's coming out

540
00:28:55,100 --> 00:28:59,100
of the laser is going to be significantly reduced

541
00:28:59,100 --> 00:29:02,100
as the laser beam is passing through this filter

542
00:29:02,100 --> 00:29:06,100
so that over on the other side, the irradiance level

543
00:29:06,100 --> 00:29:09,100
of that laser beam is pushed below

544
00:29:09,100 --> 00:29:12,100
the maximum permissible exposure.

545
00:29:12,100 --> 00:29:15,100
So the question is what would be the absorptive properties

546
00:29:15,100 --> 00:29:18,100
of this filter and also what would be the thickness

547
00:29:18,100 --> 00:29:20,100
of the filter.

548
00:29:20,100 --> 00:29:24,100
So you would want to select specific type of a filter

549
00:29:24,100 --> 00:29:27,100
or specific type of laser safety protective eyewear

550
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:31,100
with a specific number OD that is going to enable

551
00:29:31,100 --> 00:29:35,100
the reduction of the irradiance level from E sub zero

552
00:29:35,100 --> 00:29:37,100
all the way down to below NPE

553
00:29:37,100 --> 00:29:40,100
so that if you are looking into that laser beam

554
00:29:40,100 --> 00:29:45,100
with a bare eye, your bare eye is going to be protected

555
00:29:45,100 --> 00:29:49,100
by this protective, personal protective equipment

556
00:29:49,100 --> 00:29:52,100
in the form of a laser safety eyewear.

557
00:29:52,100 --> 00:29:54,100
So the way how you're going to do this calculation

558
00:29:54,100 --> 00:29:55,100
is shown down below.

559
00:29:55,100 --> 00:29:59,100
So instead of E sub x, which is going to be the output power,

560
00:29:59,100 --> 00:30:03,100
you're going to substitute maximum permissible exposure

561
00:30:03,100 --> 00:30:05,100
for this specific type of a laser.

562
00:30:05,100 --> 00:30:09,100
And if you do a few durations, you're going to come up

563
00:30:09,100 --> 00:30:12,100
with a value for the optical density or OD

564
00:30:12,100 --> 00:30:16,100
as a function of the incident irradiance levels

565
00:30:16,100 --> 00:30:21,100
from the laser and then also specific NPE number

566
00:30:21,100 --> 00:30:23,100
for that type of laser.

567
00:30:23,100 --> 00:30:25,100
If you do the calculation, you know, come up with a number

568
00:30:25,100 --> 00:30:30,100
for the OD that is going to be the minimum number OD

569
00:30:30,100 --> 00:30:34,100
that your laser safety goggles should have

570
00:30:34,100 --> 00:30:37,100
in order to properly protect your eye

571
00:30:37,100 --> 00:30:41,100
against any kind of dangerous or hazardous laser radiation.

572
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:45,100
Here we have a few different types of laser safety signs

573
00:30:45,100 --> 00:30:50,100
that are used in laser labs.

574
00:30:50,100 --> 00:30:53,100
These are slightly older signs, and you can see

575
00:30:53,100 --> 00:30:57,100
there's basically three types, danger, caution, and notice

576
00:30:57,100 --> 00:31:00,100
based on the amount of laser radiation

577
00:31:00,100 --> 00:31:02,100
or a class of laser that you're operating.

578
00:31:02,100 --> 00:31:06,100
So if you're operating class 3B and class 4 laser product,

579
00:31:06,100 --> 00:31:11,100
you're going to be using a danger sign.

580
00:31:11,100 --> 00:31:13,100
And very often on these danger signs,

581
00:31:13,100 --> 00:31:17,100
you would also have a wavelength of the laser labeled.

582
00:31:17,100 --> 00:31:20,100
And then over on the other side, on the right-hand side,

583
00:31:20,100 --> 00:31:25,100
you have two other signs, one for the lower level laser.

584
00:31:25,100 --> 00:31:28,100
So that's here showing us caution.

585
00:31:28,100 --> 00:31:31,100
Here we are talking about the laser safety sign

586
00:31:31,100 --> 00:31:33,100
for a class 2 laser product.

587
00:31:33,100 --> 00:31:37,100
And then finally, you have also notice sign

588
00:31:37,100 --> 00:31:42,100
that is going to be used for lower power levels of lasers

589
00:31:42,100 --> 00:31:45,100
that do not pose a significant laser safety risk

590
00:31:46,100 --> 00:31:48,100
for any kind of injury.

591
00:31:48,100 --> 00:31:53,100
Here we have a few laser safety signs that are newer

592
00:31:53,100 --> 00:31:54,100
or updated.

593
00:31:54,100 --> 00:31:57,100
You can see here that in addition to danger, caution,

594
00:31:57,100 --> 00:32:01,100
and notice signs, we also have other symbols

595
00:32:01,100 --> 00:32:03,100
that are being used that are explained here,

596
00:32:03,100 --> 00:32:05,100
as I already mentioned.

597
00:32:05,100 --> 00:32:08,100
These signs would very often carry the wavelength

598
00:32:08,100 --> 00:32:12,100
of the laser that they relate to.

599
00:32:12,100 --> 00:32:14,100
And then also very often there's colors

600
00:32:14,100 --> 00:32:17,100
that are being used to further indicate

601
00:32:17,100 --> 00:32:21,100
that the potential laser safety hazard

602
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:26,100
that a specific laser can pose in the lab

603
00:32:26,100 --> 00:32:29,100
that you are working in.

604
00:32:29,100 --> 00:32:32,100
How do we define these three different types

605
00:32:32,100 --> 00:32:33,100
of laser safety signs?

606
00:32:33,100 --> 00:32:36,100
It's slightly explained on this slide.

607
00:32:36,100 --> 00:32:38,100
So if you talk about caution signs,

608
00:32:38,100 --> 00:32:41,100
these signs are used for the class 2 lasers

609
00:32:41,100 --> 00:32:45,100
and some class 3A lasers depending on their irradiance.

610
00:32:45,100 --> 00:32:47,100
If we are talking about notice signs,

611
00:32:47,100 --> 00:32:51,100
these signs will be used for certain temporary laser

612
00:32:51,100 --> 00:32:54,100
controlled areas while service or repair is being done.

613
00:32:54,100 --> 00:32:57,100
And finally, danger signs are the most important ones.

614
00:32:57,100 --> 00:33:00,100
They are used for certain class 3E lasers

615
00:33:00,100 --> 00:33:02,100
of higher power levels,

616
00:33:02,100 --> 00:33:05,100
as well as for the class 3B and class 4 lasers.

617
00:33:05,100 --> 00:33:08,100
So danger signs is the most important one

618
00:33:08,100 --> 00:33:13,100
that indicates a significant laser safety risk

619
00:33:13,100 --> 00:33:15,100
that you're dealing with

620
00:33:15,100 --> 00:33:21,100
and a need for a mandatory type of laser safety protection.

621
00:33:21,100 --> 00:33:25,100
These slides present a little bit of statistics

622
00:33:25,100 --> 00:33:27,100
related to the laser accidents.

623
00:33:27,100 --> 00:33:30,100
So we can see different types of laser accidents

624
00:33:30,100 --> 00:33:35,100
and different causes of laser accidents.

625
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:39,100
And we can also see that most laser accidents occur

626
00:33:39,100 --> 00:33:45,100
because adequate control measurements are not put in place.

627
00:33:45,100 --> 00:33:48,100
And as a general statement,

628
00:33:48,100 --> 00:33:51,100
as in all aspects of laboratory field classroom

629
00:33:51,100 --> 00:33:53,100
or industrial safety,

630
00:33:53,100 --> 00:33:56,100
the best measurements would be positive attitude

631
00:33:56,100 --> 00:33:58,100
and a common sense.

632
00:33:58,100 --> 00:34:01,100
Here we have a few important safety rules

633
00:34:01,100 --> 00:34:04,100
that go under the umbrella of laser safety.

634
00:34:04,100 --> 00:34:07,100
Number one, avoid looking directly into any laser beam

635
00:34:07,100 --> 00:34:09,100
or at its reflection.

636
00:34:09,100 --> 00:34:12,100
Number two, remove all unnecessary specular

637
00:34:12,100 --> 00:34:15,100
or shiny reflecting surfaces from the work area.

638
00:34:15,100 --> 00:34:18,100
Number three, operate lasers in well-defined areas

639
00:34:18,100 --> 00:34:20,100
to which access can be controlled.

640
00:34:20,100 --> 00:34:23,100
The area should be posted with appropriate signs

641
00:34:23,100 --> 00:34:25,100
to alert persons passing by the area

642
00:34:25,100 --> 00:34:28,100
that a potential hazard exists.

643
00:34:28,100 --> 00:34:32,100
Number four, the laser system should be operated only by

644
00:34:32,100 --> 00:34:34,100
or under the direct supervision of a person

645
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:37,100
knowledgeable of the hazards and control methods

646
00:34:37,100 --> 00:34:40,100
for both beam and non-beam conditions.

647
00:34:40,100 --> 00:34:43,100
This individual is usually the laser safety officer

648
00:34:43,100 --> 00:34:44,100
or so-called LSO,

649
00:34:44,100 --> 00:34:47,100
who is designated by the administration of the company,

650
00:34:47,100 --> 00:34:50,100
hospital, or educational institution.

651
00:34:50,100 --> 00:34:52,100
The LSO shall have the authority

652
00:34:52,100 --> 00:34:55,100
and the responsibility to effect monitoring

653
00:34:55,100 --> 00:34:57,100
and enforce the control of laser hazards

654
00:34:57,100 --> 00:35:00,100
and to achieve the knowledgeable control of laser hazards.

655
00:35:00,100 --> 00:35:03,100
And finally, number five, any accidents should be reported

656
00:35:03,100 --> 00:35:06,100
immediately to the responsible medical authority

657
00:35:06,100 --> 00:35:09,100
if there is an accidental exposure to the eye,

658
00:35:09,100 --> 00:35:12,100
the service of an ophthalmologist should be solved.

659
00:35:12,100 --> 00:35:15,100
And then finally, at the end, we also want to mention

660
00:35:15,100 --> 00:35:19,100
that there's also some other non-beam-related hazards

661
00:35:19,100 --> 00:35:21,100
in the laser lab.

662
00:35:21,100 --> 00:35:23,100
We are going to mention four of them here.

663
00:35:23,100 --> 00:35:25,100
The first one is the fire hazards

664
00:35:25,100 --> 00:35:28,100
for certain types of lasers, especially Class 4,

665
00:35:29,100 --> 00:35:33,100
that can cause the fire if not being used in a proper way.

666
00:35:33,100 --> 00:35:36,100
Number two is the explosion hazard

667
00:35:36,100 --> 00:35:38,100
for certain types of lasers,

668
00:35:38,100 --> 00:35:40,100
such as high-pressure arc lamps, filament lamps,

669
00:35:40,100 --> 00:35:43,100
and capacitor banks in the laser equipment.

670
00:35:43,100 --> 00:35:45,100
The third one is electrical hazard

671
00:35:45,100 --> 00:35:47,100
because you are dealing with electrical equipment.

672
00:35:47,100 --> 00:35:50,100
And finally, the fourth non-beam hazard is a chemical hazard

673
00:35:50,100 --> 00:35:54,100
for a specific type of laser, such as dye lasers.

674
00:35:54,100 --> 00:35:56,100
This concludes the last lecture in our introduction

675
00:35:56,100 --> 00:35:58,100
to photonics video series, and then also concludes

676
00:35:58,100 --> 00:36:02,100
the theoretical part of this course.

677
00:36:02,100 --> 00:36:05,100
You have acquired a certain set of skills,

678
00:36:05,100 --> 00:36:07,100
at least on a theoretical level,

679
00:36:07,100 --> 00:36:10,100
that are important and fundamental

680
00:36:10,100 --> 00:36:13,100
to be able to work in a photonics lab

681
00:36:13,100 --> 00:36:16,100
as a photonics technician.

682
00:36:16,100 --> 00:36:19,100
The next part would be the hands-on experience

683
00:36:19,100 --> 00:36:22,100
where you're going to be conducting

684
00:36:22,100 --> 00:36:24,100
certain experiments in the lab

685
00:36:24,100 --> 00:36:27,100
that would further strengthen your set of skills

686
00:36:27,100 --> 00:36:31,100
that you learn here through the theory.

687
00:36:31,100 --> 00:36:32,100
At the end of this course,

688
00:36:32,100 --> 00:36:35,100
I also would like to bring to your attention

689
00:36:35,100 --> 00:36:40,100
three other courses that are part of this online curriculum.

